{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## 6|''^### 6|B/S/T|provenance'\"' collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## 6|''^### 6|B/S/T|provenance'\"' collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Call-site provenance: occurrence contract and durable correspondence\n12:Adopt one language-neutral application-occurrence contract over the existing occurrence relation, one reserved frontend-metadata key, and the already-ruled third trace field. Keep code content, provenance, attribution evidence, and verification results separate. Publish the durable correspondence as an additional addressable record referring to the oracle's evidence **without changing that evidence's format**. Production adoption includes Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, Python, their preludes, and the additional ingress paths inventoried below.\n45:Dedicated operations such as `:dao.stream.apply/call`, stream nodes, and continuation nodes retain existing instruction provenance. They are not silently counted as `:application` sites. Their adapters' application occurrences are covered; an extension to count other node kinds must explicitly version the coverage policy (`vm.cljc:1015-1028`; `semantic/linearize.cljc:126-139`).\n73:Persist the existing instruction relation `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`. The row linearizer already returns `{:address ... :provenance [[pc origin root path] ...]}`; its caller supplies the segment coordinate (`semantic/linearize.cljc:188-214`; code-as-tuples §5.3 at lines 918–931). A call dispatch looks up its **own call instruction's** emitting occurrence, not the preceding operand's instruction or the callee's body. Multiple instructions may belong to one occurrence.\n75:Segment+PC alone is insufficient when identical code is loaded under two origins. The runtime must retain its load/admission origin alongside its image reference when selecting provenance. This is an occurrence-scoped runtime context, not a new code hash. The native image derivations must supply or independently reconstruct a mapping from their call PCs back through semantic PCs to K; PC equality across formats must not be assumed. Current semantic `resolve` preserves PCs, register derivation preserves layout, and stack derivation emits a new sequence (`semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-93,154-172`).\n116:| Code lowering profiles | Read, and verify unchanged code output. A separately versioned provenance recipe names any new side mapping. | Revise the relevant code profile if a pinned lowering rule or emitted code changes, even if execution grammar does not. A side-only addition must not masquerade as validation by the old derivation verifier. |\n121:| Continuation/UCF/checkpoint contracts | Read/inventory initially. | If a provenance-dependent consumer requires new serialized runtime state, inventory and version that actual state contract before shipping. A transient field cannot be silently serialized or relied on after migration. |\n141:| Macro pipeline: `yin/vm/macro.cljc:997-1005,1062-1119,1196-1261` | Keep expansion attempt provenance, resolve final-root ownership, annotate generated occurrences and retain retrievable expansion records. Post-expansion is a required integration point. |\n144:| Canonical and datom reconstruction: `yin/vm.cljc:705,818-851,1477-1555` | Side metadata does not reappear from content alone. Attach provenance context out of band; do not collapse occurrence ids via shared reconstruction/entity ids. Round-trip canonical hashes must remain equal. |\n145:| Semantic row and datom lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc:106-128,188-221,283-302` | Persist returned provenance, supply origins explicitly, bridge datom entities with occurrences; nil-origin legacy calls remain loadable but cannot satisfy C1 attribution. |\n146:| Native lowering: `yin/vm/semantic/derive.cljc:21-42,60-120,154-184` | Produce/reconstruct complete native-call-PC provenance; compose with semantic mapping. Sparse variable/closure diagnostics do not establish call coverage. Inventory any legacy direct de Bruijn lowering still reached after merges. |\n148:| Existing derivation verification: `yin/vm/ledger.cljc:156-215`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc:2164-2255` | Existing verification re-lowers code and compares identities, not side metadata. Keep code verification and add independent provenance/correspondence verification. `:trusted` does not satisfy this new acceptance gate. |\n149:| Generic publication: `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:115-218` | Currently takes only `:vector` from `project-rows` at line 157. Publish complete side artifacts and origin/record closure beside code; do not discard provenance or change manifest keys casually. Validate before making a C1 availability claim. |\n152:| Code index / republishing: `yin/repl/ast_index.cljc:3-24,108`; `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:225-300` | Query views can be rebuilt from rows but cannot recover omitted admission provenance. Session/index-to-module publication must preserve or explicitly remap occurrence roots, and retain original evidence sources. |\n157:“Side tables are additive” is the compatibility policy, not permission to skip republishing coverage evidence. **Unannotated persisted artifacts remain loadable under their supported existing code contracts.** A provenance-requiring query returns missing provenance/refuses credit; it never invents identity from names, hashes, arities, values or source lines. Old provenance may be reconstructed only when the actual admission/expansion inputs are retained, and the result is a newly verified artifact.\n162:2. For each host publication: canonical row closure, semantic vector, stack/register stored images and checksums, derivation records, schema-1 manifest, pinned dependency addresses, new C1 side tables/provenance/correspondence, and discovery/index/name/signature records where used. The actual publication sequence is `yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:156-209`. Side-only annotation should leave old code addresses unchanged; measure this rather than assume it. New side objects still need publication, retrieval and retention.\n172:Preserve the existing failure categories: malformed canonical content fails structural validation; wrong bytes/address fails hash verification; unknown execution contract refuses load; differing lowering profile reports profile mismatch or `:unverified-derivation`; wrong recomputed output reports derivation mismatch (`vm.cljc:283-285`; `ledger.cljc:156-180`; `linker.cljc:2175-2215`). New evidence-specific failures must be distinguishable: unsupported C1/evidence version, missing provenance, bad occurrence, incomplete coverage, wrong source/adaptation hash, missing lane, ambiguous correspondence, stale declared site, or reconstruction mismatch.\n174:A corrupted required provenance artifact refuses the provenance-dependent operation even if code itself verifies. An absent optional annotation does not make old code unloadable. A signature authenticates a publisher; it does not verify a correspondence. A trusted code link is not evidence of a recomputed correspondence.\n196: :yang.correspondence/instruction-provenance\n197: [<Jing address of an instruction-provenance relation> ...]}\n200:The D1 key set is closed; vectors preserve declared order and contain no duplicate declarations. Empty artifact/provenance vectors are allowed for a tree-only consumer, whose claimed coverage must say tree-only. `artifacts` lists lowered images only; canonical roots are retrievable row closures listed separately. Native image identity need not equal its storage address: the publisher already distinguishes checksums and stored images (`publish.cljc:159-185,204-208`). The record's address is the ordinary Jing content address of the whole map; no self-address field.\n217:The recipe profile pins frontend/compiler revision, grammar/options, expansion environment and recorded origins/attempts, all code-producing transformations, source canonicalization, site-table derivation, and lowering/provenance rules. The consumer must implement that exact recipe/profile. A missing upstream canonicalization rule is not repaired by choosing a plausible hash. The exact oracle schema identity remains the dependency in §1.1.\n227:1. Fetch D1, recipe, OracleEvidence and every required source/row/provenance artifact; verify their addresses and structural schemas. Reject an unsupported oracle schema before interpreting any row.\n243:Each adoption brief must cite this findings file and C1/D1 by revision. The shared occurrence finalizer/validator and provenance publication work precede or accompany frontend implementation; D1 independent verification precedes acceptance of the first frontend consumer that relies on D1. A frontend may adopt static annotation without consuming oracle attribution, but cannot call that durable correspondence acceptance.\n258:On each host run static occurrence/provenance checks for AST, semantic, stack and register artifacts where the existing format admits the fixture. Execute direct walker and all three vector paths for the portable correctness corpus. Linked paths exercise all supported linked formats and pin existing unsupported/refusal cases explicitly. For example, the inspected TS linked harness documents a walker `:undeclared-free` limitation while using three vector VMs (`test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_harness.cljc:11-15`); this design does not silently promise that old unrelated limitation is fixed. If a missing lane prevents the required new provenance test, report adoption incomplete with the named dependency.\n267:- Identical C1 rows, reconstructed site declarations, and per-format instruction provenance. Different formats have different PCs, but they must resolve to the same intended application K.\n284:Each brief's acceptance bundle contains: exact producer/artifact checklist; input/commit/profile pins; C1 coverage counts and root/hash invariance; four-host results and cross-host equality report; correctness battery results; before/after budgets and measurements; legacy-load/missing-provenance refusal; and D1 verification result if it relies on correspondence. No frontend can satisfy this bundle merely by running the oracle test.\n286:## 6. Measurement and declared budgets\n290:### 6.1 Counts and cost model\n295:- `P_f`: instruction count/provenance-row count for format f; report shared semantic/native mapping structures separately if used.\n296:- `S_static`: exact encoded bytes of C1 rows, provenance tables, retained origin records, D1 and its reconstruction inputs; distinguish fresh bytes from already retained shared content, index memory and storage/dedup savings.\n302:### 6.2 Experiment separation\n307:2. **S — static annotation:** C1/provenance production, persistence, loading and query enabled; runtime trace disabled.\n314:### 6.3 Bounds published before any tagged run\n331:### 6.4 Budget impact allocated per frontend\n340:The storage/work formulas are the design's budget impact; numbers require the pinned post-sweep corpus and are not asserted here. Shared `yang.call` must be reported once as retained shared content and separately as each frontend's dependency, avoiding both double charging and disappearance. Prelude republishing on four hosts is mandatory even when code hashes remain unchanged: the new occurrence/provenance artifacts need independent production/load evidence.\n344:Every gate compares trace-disabled program outcomes first, then occurrence/provenance correctness, then any attribution. A marker is not proof of execution. These extend the signed oracle battery; they do not replace its seven cases and stale-table diagnostic (`ruling-of-record.md:23-34,64-69`).\n372:2. After the cell-ref sweeps/J2 land, refresh the exact producer, validator, artifact and host inventory; identify every changed contract, every read-only dependency, and any required format revision. Resolve final expansion-root ownership and native provenance completeness.\n381:- **Preservation is not currently end-to-end:** encoder replacement/empty-table paths, safepoint clearing, publication's discarded provenance and content-shared reconstruction are concrete integration hazards. Native and continuation paths need their own reconstruction evidence.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '286,331p' collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md && cat test/yang/correspondence_static.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '286,331p' collab/yang-clojure/1794670000000-architect-callsite-generalization-design.gpt-6-astra.findings.md && cat test/yang/correspondence_static.clj\"","aggregated_output":"## 6. Measurement and declared budgets\n\nThese are measurement requirements and design cost models, **not measured performance claims**. Signed A3 requires a budget impact per frontend, and line 29 requires static/runtime separation. This read-only design has no authority to invent benchmark results. Numeric per-corpus totals remain **unknown — the implementation round's inventory decides by measuring the pinned baseline before tagged runs**.\n\n### 6.1 Counts and cost model\n\nFor each fixture/prelude/module, host, and format record:\n\n- `U`: distinct canonical rows; `O`: all occurrence-tree positions; `A`: `:application` occurrences; `D`: maximum path depth; `L`: total encoded length of application paths; separate user, helper, wrapper, prelude, macro-generated and Rule-R counts as derived diagnostic groups.\n- `P_f`: instruction count/provenance-row count for format f; report shared semantic/native mapping structures separately if used.\n- `S_static`: exact encoded bytes of C1 rows, provenance tables, retained origin records, D1 and its reconstruction inputs; distinguish fresh bytes from already retained shared content, index memory and storage/dedup savings.\n- `E`: actual callee-entry records, per-lane total bytes and maximum record size; `Q_peak`: peak retained trace records and bytes, dropped/blocked writes and drain schedule.\n- Guest instruction counts and host-side work counters; wall compile/expand/project/publish/verify/load/query time; process/heap peak and retained memory, including trace buffers, indexes and pending continuations.\n\nOccurrence counts can greatly exceed unique row counts because identical subtrees are shared; an `O(U)` annotation-size promise would be false (code-as-tuples `:393-404,636-665,947-949`). A direct marker representation costs one row per A plus encoded paths/origins: storage proportional to `A + L + coordinate bytes`; traversal work proportional to visited occurrences, not unique content. Instruction mapping adds up to `P_f` rows per claimed format with path/coordinate payload. The exact peak depends on streaming vs retaining both trees/tables and must be measured.\n\n### 6.2 Experiment separation\n\nUse the same pinned code-producing inputs and three configurations:\n\n1. **B — baseline:** existing code, trace disabled.\n2. **S — static annotation:** C1/provenance production, persistence, loading and query enabled; runtime trace disabled.\n3. **T — tagged tracing:** same S outputs plus caller propagation and body-entry recording.\n\nAlso compare the oracle's existing two-field tracing with T to isolate the extra caller-site field from the original body-entry instrumentation. Report S−B and T−S separately, and the old-trace→tagged-trace delta. If instrumentation itself changes code, keep its canonical image and compile cost separate from side-only annotation. Static C1 must change **zero canonical code bytes/addresses and zero guest instructions** for otherwise identical input; runtime tracing has no such zero-cost claim.\n\nMeasure cold and warm compile/load/query separately; use fresh publication caches for cold tests, fixed workload/repetition count and host/version, and publish distribution plus raw samples rather than an unqualified single timing. Report query indexes/build time separately from query execution. Do not call a host trace hook “zero overhead” because guest instruction count did not increase.\n\n### 6.3 Bounds published before any tagged run\n\nThe implementation preflight record must contain actual finite numbers (no “TBD”, unlimited trace, or post-run adjustment) for:\n\n| Bound | Required derivation / failure |\n|---|---|\n| Maximum input rows, occurrences, A, D and total path bytes | Derive from the pinned static corpus; reject over-bound traversal before instrumentation. Include generated/bundled prelude expansion, not only source calls. |\n| Static side bytes and retained memory | Measure S without tracing; declare maximum per artifact/module and aggregate closure. Serialization size may be computed exactly before execution. Stop/revise on exceeding the declared storage/memory bound. |\n| Maximum records E and trace capacity C | Bound accepted trace writes for the fixture, declare C in records AND bytes including worst allowed site-id/path length, and declare drain behavior. No credit from a truncated/overflowed trace. |\n| Guest instruction ceiling and added work | Bound trace instructions per event/dispatch under each executed format, plus fixed setup/drain costs. Declare a ceiling for each host/format run before T; report baseline, absolute delta and ratio. |\n| Compile/expand/publish/verify/load/query time | Declare finite absolute time limits and allowed deltas against B/S in the preflight record. Pilot B/S measurements may set these; a tagged timeout is a failed bounded run. |\n| Peak process/heap/trace/index/continuation memory | Declare finite ceilings and measurement method; if a host lacks a heap counter, use a named process-memory measure and mark comparability limits. Missing measurement is not zero usage. |\n\nFor the oracle specifically, current code uses trace capacity **1,000,000 records**, drains after halt, and allows **4,000,000 guest instructions per C lane** (`latte_oracle_test.clj:1827,1847-1854,2453-2454`). These are the inspected baseline settings, not evidence the tagged run fits. Its own note records 2,024,912 walker and 1,105,792 semantic steps for the older binding fixture at line 2454. Restate both ceilings, the new record byte bound and the occupancy argument before T. If the implementation cannot establish enough capacity under those ceilings, publish revised bounds and their justification before running tagged evidence. Never silently raise fuel to get credit, or let a full trace stream park the fixture and misclassify it as semantic failure.\n\nFor other frontends, no oracle 4M/1M setting is inherited by default. Their adoption preflight must give numeric ceilings for each required fixture/prelude/host and format. Exceeding a declared bound blocks that acceptance run; changing bounds creates a new versioned run declaration and preserves the failed run.\n\n### 6.4 Budget impact allocated per frontend\n(ns yang.correspondence-static\n  \"Static C1 production, storage and query measurement; not D1 verification.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]\n            [yang.correspondence-verifier :as verifier]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\n\n\n(defn measure-static!\n  \"For a retained tree and recorded origin, rederive C1 rows, compare them to\n   supplied metadata, materialize and fetch both, then query C1 markers. The\n   handle is caller-supplied so experiments can use fresh stores. No guest runs.\"\n  [tree origin metadata handle clock]\n  (when-not (vm/occurrence-origin? origin)\n    (throw (ex-info \"Invalid occurrence origin\" {:stage :admission})))\n  (let [elapsed (fn [t] (quot (+ (- (clock) t) 999999) 1000000))\n        t (clock)\n        baseline (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast tree))\n        baseline-ms (elapsed t)\n        _ (when-not (= tree baseline)\n            (throw (ex-info \"Baseline row projection differs\" {:stage :root})))\n        t (clock)\n        applications (vm/application-occurrences tree)\n        markers (mapv (fn [[root path]] [origin root path :yang/call-site 1])\n                      (sort-by pr-str applications))\n        supplied (filterv #(and (vector? %) (= 5 (count %))\n                                (= :yang/call-site (nth % 3))) metadata)\n        _ (when-not (and (= (count supplied) (count (set supplied)))\n                         (= (set markers) (set supplied)))\n            (throw (ex-info \"C1 markers differ\" {:stage :metadata})))\n        marker-ms (elapsed t)\n        t (clock)\n        root (vm/materialize-tree! handle tree)\n        marker-address (jing/materialize! handle markers)\n        publish-ms (elapsed t)\n        t (clock)\n        loaded-tree (verifier/fetch-tree! handle root (inc (count (:rows tree))))\n        loaded-markers (jing/get handle marker-address ::missing)\n        _ (when-not (and (= tree loaded-tree) (= markers loaded-markers))\n            (throw (ex-info \"C1 content differs after fetch\" {:stage :load})))\n        load-ms (elapsed t)\n        t (clock)\n        relation (query/relation (mapv (fn [[o r p a value]] [[o r p] a value 0 nil]) loaded-markers))\n        relation-ms (elapsed t)\n        t (clock)\n        found (set (query/collect (query/q '[:find ?e :where [?e :yang/call-site 1 _ _]] relation)))\n        expected (set (map (fn [[o r p]] [[o r p]]) loaded-markers))\n        _ (when-not (= expected found)\n            (throw (ex-info \"C1 marker query differs\" {:stage :query})))\n        query-ms (elapsed t)]\n    {:status :pass :root root :marker-address marker-address :marker-count (count markers)\n     :marker-bytes (alength (cbor/encode markers))\n     :phases {:baseline-project-ms baseline-ms :marker-derive-ms marker-ms\n              :publish-ms publish-ms :load-ms load-ms :relation-build-ms relation-ms :query-ms query-ms}}))\n\n\n(defn compare-static!\n  \"Measure baseline tree publication/retrieval in one fresh store and C1\n   rederivation/publication/retrieval/query in another. Both use the exact\n   same retained root; no guest executes and no new source origin is minted.\"\n  [tree origin metadata baseline-store c1-store clock]\n  (when (identical? baseline-store c1-store)\n    (throw (ex-info \"Baseline and C1 stores must be distinct\" {:stage :configuration})))\n  (let [elapsed (fn [t] (quot (+ (- (clock) t) 999999) 1000000))\n        t (clock)\n        root (vm/materialize-tree! baseline-store tree)\n        publish-ms (elapsed t)\n        t (clock)\n        loaded (verifier/fetch-tree! baseline-store root (inc (count (:rows tree))))\n        _ (when-not (= tree loaded)\n            (throw (ex-info \"Baseline tree differs after reload\" {:stage :load})))\n        load-ms (elapsed t)\n        result (measure-static! tree origin metadata c1-store clock)]\n    (when-not (= root (:root result))\n      (throw (ex-info \"Static C1 changed code root\" {:stage :root})))\n    (assoc result :baseline-phases {:publish-ms publish-ms :load-ms load-ms})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
